  "Name": "Dobrowolski1970-fig2-filter1",
  "Source": "J. A. Dobrowolski, \"Optical Interference Filters for the Adjustment of Spectral Response and Spectral Power Distribution,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 1396-1402 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.001396 - Fig. 2 caption, filter 1: (L.3H)^2(L.5H)^2(L.7H)^2(L.9H)^2(L.11H.L), p. 1397; text p. 1398 (Sec. III.A, xenon line at 0.467 um) and Fig. 5",
  "Notes": "Narrow-band rejection filter to suppress the 0.467 um xenon arc emission line in a xenon lamp + glass filter D65 simulator (Sec. III.A)\nSpecification: Reduce the xenon emission line at 0.467 um to the background level while leaving the rest of 0.3-0.7 um unchanged.\nReported: Fig. 2 curve 1: measured transmittance (uncorrected for surface reflection losses), high (~0.8-0.9) across 0.33-0.7 um with a shallow rejection near 0.467 um; Fig. 5: relative intensity of the xenon lamp around the line drops from a peak of ~3.8 (curve 1, before) to ~2.5 (curve 2, after), i.e. the rejection is roughly 30-35 % (plot readings, no printed numbers).\nNotes: 19 layers: (L 3H)(L 3H)(L 5H)(L 5H)(L 7H)(L 7H)(L 9H)(L 9H) L 11H L, in QWOT; the dots in the caption are separators, 3H = three quarter waves of H. n_H = 1.50, n_L = 1.38, substrate and medium 1.46 (Fig. 2 caption: substrate and medium 1.52 for all filters except filter 1, 1.46). ASSUMPTION: the reference (quarter-wave) wavelength is not printed; 467 nm is taken from the text, which says filter 1 was built to suppress the emission band at 0.467 um - all odd-order layers then reject at that wavelength. Which end faces the light is not stated; with equal, lossless media on both sides R/T are the same either way. Measured curve is uncorrected for the two outer surfaces. Normal incidence assumed.\nLayers from Fig. 2 caption, filter 1: (L.3H)^2(L.5H)^2(L.7H)^2(L.9H)^2(L.11H.L), p. 1397; text p. 1398 (Sec. III.A, xenon line at 0.467 um) and Fig. 5; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
